showed `c2GJK` mismatches — but **only** when I fed it a hand-fabricated `c2GJKCache`. I chased it down rather than papering over it: a cache may name vertex indices `1..3`, while `c2MakeProxy` fills only `verts[0]` for a circle, so C reads **uninitialized stack**. Proof that this is indeterminate in C and unmatchable by any translation: the *same C source* built at `-O0` vs `-O3` disagrees with itself on 1 048 375 of those checks. With a properly managed cache (zeroed, then self-populated and reused — the documented usage), C, C, and Rust agree exactly.\n\n## Faithfulness notes\n\nC quirks were reproduced, not fixed: `c2MakeProxy` leaves the proxy untouched for an unknown type; `c2GJKSimplexMetric`/`c2D`/`c2L` keep their `default:` fallthroughs; struct-copy ordering in `c22`/`c23` is preserved; `ptr_from_parts` calls the real libc `malloc` (so callers can `free` the result) and I kept its missing-`return` path as a null pointer — indeterminate in C, but provably never dereferenced, since `c2Collided` returns 0 for an unknown type before touching the pointer. `c2Simplex`'s `a,b,c,d` members are modeled as `[c2sv; 4]` to mirror the C code's `c2sv *verts = &s.a;` pointer walk, and I verified every struct size/offset against the C compiler (`c2sv` 36, `c2Simplex` 152, `c2Capsule` 20 → MEMORY-class, etc.) before writing any Rust.\n\nScratch build artifacts have been cleaned up; `c_src/` is untouched."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":941,"cache_read_input_tokens":83966,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":941,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"edefcec1-fbb3-4505-aade-74b6b527f26b","uuid":"e25e8e61-a65c-4d7f-87d6-0d7797110094","timestamp":"2026-08-27T11:42:35.393Z"}
